{
  // Node here and not in `tsconfig.json`, because `src` never touches one.
  // The tests read the package's own source off disk and run the panel's text
  // in a `node:vm` context, so they are Node programs the way every other
  // package's tests are. DOM comes along because `dom.ts` writes out the
  // handful of members the panel reaches for, and the shapes it stands in for
  // are named in the panel's own annotations.
  //
  // `src` is included alongside `test`, which the other packages' test
  // projects do not need to do. Theirs arrives through the imports their tests
  // write, and so does most of this one's — `click.test.ts` imports
  // `../src/background.js`. What does not arrive that way is
  // `src/chrome.d.ts`: an ambient declaration is a file nothing imports, which
  // is the whole of what makes it ambient, so a project that does not name it
  // does not have it. Including only `test` pulled `background.ts` in through
  // a test and left the declaration of `chrome` out, and this project failed
  // with `Cannot find name 'chrome'` on a clean checkout while passing after a
  // build had left a `dist` behind to resolve against.
  //
  // The whole directory rather than the one declaration, so the next `.d.ts`
  // to land here is covered without anyone remembering to come back.
  // `programs.test.ts` holds that, and holds it against the configuration that
  // shipped.
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["test", "src"]
}
